# ---- Arkansas ----
N('eureka-springs-carnegie-library-ar','Eureka Springs Carnegie Public Library','Eureka Springs','ar','civic','standing',36.40744,-93.73669,'exact','open','walk','','1910–1912',
  "A library of lightly rusticated local limestone with Tuscan columns and a red clay tile roof, set into the steep hillside so that a double stair climbs to it from the street as part of a tall retaining wall. George W. Hellmuth of St. Louis designed it, on a site given by R. C. Kerens, an investor in the Crescent Hotel up the hill. It was built from 1910 and finished in 1912; the grant was $12,500, and $3,000 more came after the board president pleaded for it. Short of money, it closed in the winter of 1916 and reopened in 1920. It is still the public library, and its basement is part of the town's underground tour. On the National Register.",
  'Local limestone, with a double stair up from the street.',
  '','194 Spring Street (one source says 198). A working public library.',
  [EOA,['SAH Archipedia: Carnegie Public Library, Eureka Springs','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/49294'],['Eureka Springs Carnegie Public Library','https://eurekalibrary.org/'],['HMdb: Eureka Springs Carnegie Public Library','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=80137'],LA_],
  brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Mon–Thu 9–7, Fri–Sat 9–5, Sun 12–5'),
N('fort-smith-carnegie-library-ar','Fort Smith Carnegie Library (later KFSM-TV)','Fort Smith','ar','civic','standing',35.38564,-94.41578,'exact','view','walk','','1907–1908',
  "The Fortnightly Club, a women's club, worked for a library from 1891, and this one, modelled on the Carnegie library at Guthrie, Oklahoma, was dedicated on January 30, 1908, on the site of Judge Isaac C. Parker's house, which an 1898 tornado had wrecked; the land was bought from his widow. The cornerstone holds a time capsule. The library moved to a new building in 1969 or 1970, and the building became the KFSM-TV studio until 2019. A preservationist bought it at auction in 2024 and plans to restore it as close to the original as possible, uncovering the beams, arches, mosaic and glass-block floors and the skylight over the rotunda. It is not on the National Register.",
  'A rotunda with a skylight, hidden under the television station\'s changes.',
  '','318 North 13th Street, at D Street. Privately owned: look from the street.',
  [['Fort Smith Public Library: history','https://fortsmithlibrary.org/about-the-library/about-us/fspl-history'],['Talk Business: restoration plans for the Carnegie Library building (2025)','https://talkbusiness.net/2025/01/restoration-plans-begin-with-the-historic-carnegie-library-building/'],EOA,LA_],brick='?'),
N('little-rock-carnegie-library-site-ar','Site of the Little Rock Carnegie Library','Little Rock','ar','civic','gone',34.74234,-92.27270,'area','view','walk','','1908–1963',
  "A wide stone library at the southwest corner of West 7th and Louisiana streets, on land in the city park, opened on February 1, 1910, after the grant was raised from $50,000 to $88,100. The Encyclopedia of Arkansas credits Edward Tilton of New York with Charles Thompson of Little Rock directing the work. It was torn down for urban renewal in 1963 or 1964, and the 1964 library went up on about the same spot. Four of its stone columns were saved and have stood since 2009 in front of the Main Library in the River Market district.",
  'Four of its columns stand in front of the Main Library in the River Market.',
  '','West 7th and South Louisiana streets. Nothing of the library stands here.',
  [EOA,['Encyclopedia of Arkansas: Central Arkansas Library System','https://encyclopediaofarkansas.net/entries/central-arkansas-library-system-3797/'],['Arkansas Democrat-Gazette: building a library (2020)','https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2020/mar/18/building-a-library-20200318/'],LA_],brick='?'),
N('morrilton-carnegie-library-ar','Morrilton Carnegie Library (Conway County Library)','Morrilton','ar','civic','standing',35.15113,-92.74542,'exact','open','walk','','1915–1916',
  "The Pathfinder Club, a women's club of 1897, won the grant, and the red brick library by Thomas Harding opened in October 1916, 30 by 50 feet, with four large arched windows across the front and a heavy red tile hipped roof. The Encyclopedia of Arkansas says Morrilton was the smallest town in America to have a Carnegie library when it opened. The lower floor joined the library in 1972, and an addition with an elevator came in 2000. It is still the Conway County Library. On the National Register since 1978.",
  'Red brick, with four large arched windows and a red tile roof.',
  '','101 West Church Street, at Division Street. A working public library.',
  [W('Conway County Library'),['Conway County Library: about','https://conwaycountylibrary.org/about-the-library/'],['National Register: Conway County Library','https://www.arkansasheritage.com/docs/default-source/national-registry/CN0165-pdf'],LA_],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='Mon–Fri 10–6, Sat 10–2'),
# ---- Louisiana ----
N('alexandria-carnegie-library-la','Alexandria Carnegie Library (Louisiana History Museum)','Alexandria','la','civic','standing',31.30908,-92.44469,'exact','open','walk','','1907–',
  "S. S. Bryan of Alexandria and Andrew Carnegie each offered $10,000, and Crosby & Henkel of New Orleans designed a one-story Beaux-Arts library of brick laid in red mortar on a stuccoed raised basement, with terra-cotta capitals and a terra-cotta doorway carved with an open book, flaming torches and a scallop shell. Inside are a Greek key tile floor and false beams. The windows have been replaced. Since 1971 it has been the Louisiana History Museum, with the genealogical library upstairs, in a building the city owns. On the National Register since 1989.",
  'A terra-cotta doorway with an open book, torches and a shell.',
  '','503 Washington Street. The museum is free.',
  [W('Louisiana History Museum'),['Louisiana History Museum: contact','https://www.louisianahistorymuseum.org/contact-us'],LL],brick='?',hours='Tue–Sat 10–4'),
N('jennings-carnegie-library-la','Jennings Carnegie Public Library','Jennings','la','civic','standing',30.22289,-92.65961,'exact','open','walk','','1907–1908',
  "Pushed for by the Ladies Library Association of 1885 and built in 1908 to designs by Whitfield & King of New York (Henry D. Whitfield was Carnegie's brother-in-law). It is V-shaped: an octagonal domed rotunda with a skylight at the entrance and two arched wings set at 45 degrees, of buff brick with white wood trim under red tile roofs, with a Serlian doorway framed by four Corinthian columns. Archipedia likens it to Jefferson's Poplar Forest. The wings were remodelled in 1952. It is still the city library. On the National Register since 1982.",
  'Buff brick wings either side of a domed octagonal entrance.',
  '','303 North Cary Avenue. A working public library run by the city.',
  [W('Jennings Carnegie Public Library'),['City of Jennings: Carnegie Public Library','https://www.cityofjennings.com/carnegie-public-library/'],['SAH Archipedia: Jennings Carnegie Public Library','https://archipediavm.upress.virginia.edu/buildings/LA-01-JD2'],LL],
  brick='no',bcol='buff',hours='Mon–Sat 9–5'),
N('new-orleans-carnegie-library-site-la','Site of the New Orleans Carnegie main library, Lee Circle','New Orleans','la','civic','gone',29.94304,-90.07292,'area','view','walk','','1906–1959',
  "The city's main library, by Diboll, Owen & Goldstein, was opened in October 1908 on St. Charles Avenue at Lee Circle (now Égalité Circle): 160 feet long under a 78-foot copper dome, of Bedford stone with four 30-foot limestone columns, modelled on the Temple of Mars Ultor in Rome. It was open to white readers only. When the Loyola Avenue library opened in December 1958 the old one was sold at auction and torn down in 1959; an office building took its place.",
  '',
  '','1031 St. Charles Avenue, at the circle. Nothing of the library stands.',
  [['The Historic New Orleans Collection: lost New Orleans landmarks','https://hnoc.org/publishing/first-draft/stories-behind-16-lost-new-orleans-landmarks'],['New Orleans Magazine: 1908 New Orleans Public Library','https://www.myneworleans.com/1908-new-orleans-public-library/'],NOA,LL],brick='no'),
N('algiers-carnegie-library-la','Algiers Carnegie Library (Cita Dennis Hubbell Library)','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95311,-90.04823,'exact','open','walk','','1907–',
  "The Algiers Point branch opened on December 28, 1907. Decay and damage from Hurricane Betsy closed it in 1966; a campaign led by Cita Dennis Hubbell reopened it in 1975, and it was named for her in 2002. Closed again in 2008 for fear the roof would fall in, it reopened in 2013. The library calls it the city's oldest working library location.",
  '',
  '','725 Pelican Avenue, Algiers Point. A working branch library.',
  [['New Orleans Public Library: Cita Dennis Hubbell Library','https://nolalibrary.org/2021/09/17/cita-dennis-hubbell-library-a-neighborhood-treasure-since-1907/'],W('New Orleans Public Library'),NOA,LL],brick='?',hours='Mon–Thu 10–7, Fri–Sat 10–5'),
N('canal-carnegie-library-la','Canal Street Carnegie Library','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.96734,-90.09058,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1911',
  "A two-story Arts and Crafts branch by Lagarde and Burk, dedicated on August 26, 1911, with paired stairs rising from the entrance. It closed as a library in 1958. A 50-foot Works Progress Administration mural of 1941, The History of Printing by Edward T. Schoenberger, was uncovered and restored in a 2010 renovation, after Katrina put four feet of water in the building, and a yoga studio moved in. It is privately owned.",
  'Paired stairs rising from the entrance.',
  '','2940 Canal Street, near Gayoso Street. Privately owned: look from the street.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Canal Street branch library','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/LA-02-OR91'],['Living New Deal: Canal Street Branch Library mural','https://livingnewdeal.org/sites/canal-street-branch-library-mural-new-orleans-la/'],NOA,LL],brick='?'),
N('dryades-branch-library-la','Dryades Street Branch Library (Carnegie)','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.93608,-90.08443,'site','view','walk','','1912–1965',
  "The first library for Black New Orleanians, pushed for by James Hardy Dillard and the Reverend Robert E. Jones with Booker T. Washington's support, and opened in 1915 (one city page says 1913). William R. Burk designed it in red brick with Bedford limestone, its front modelled on a triumphal arch of three round-arched bays with Ionic pilasters, an open book and wreaths. The NAACP and the Negro Board of Trade met there. Hurricane Betsy and a fire closed it in 1965. A YMCA uses it now. On the National Register.",
  'Red brick and limestone, its front a triumphal arch.',
  '','1924 Philip Street, at Oretha Castle Haley Boulevard. A YMCA: look from the street.',
  [W('Dryades Branch Library'),['SAH Archipedia: Dryades Branch Library','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/LA-02-OR152'],NOA,LL],brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('napoleon-carnegie-library-la','Napoleon Avenue Carnegie Library (Children\'s Resource Center Library)','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.92135,-90.10106,'exact','open','walk','','1907–1908',
  "Dedicated on January 31, 1908, a branch by Favrot and Livaudais with brick walls in Flemish bond and limestone quoins and cornice. Its 1978 nomination calls it the only New Orleans Carnegie library still intact. In 1963 the FBI searched its books after Lee Harvey Oswald had borrowed some there. It is now the library's early-literacy branch for children under five; it closed for renovation in May 2025, and the library says it has reopened.",
  'Brick in Flemish bond with limestone quoins.',
  '','913 Napoleon Avenue, between Camp and Magazine streets. A working branch library for young children.',
  [['New Orleans Public Library: early literacy','https://nolalibrary.org/youth/early-literacy/'],['National Register nomination: Napoleon Avenue Branch Library','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/70f33b82-c7a7-40d4-a72a-98b17944bda8'],NOA,LL],brick='?',hours='Mon–Thu 10–6, Fri–Sat 10–5'),
N('royal-carnegie-library-site-la','Site of the Royal Street Carnegie Library','New Orleans','la','civic','gone',29.96442,-90.05766,'area','view','walk','','1907–1965',
  "A Carnegie branch in the Marigny near Washington Square, dedicated on November 25, 1907. It was badly damaged by Hurricane Betsy in 1965 and torn down.",
  '',
  '','2100 Royal Street, the address the city archives give. Nothing of the library stands.',
  [W('New Orleans Public Library'),NOA,LL],brick='?'),
# ---- Mississippi ----
N('clarksdale-carnegie-library-ms','Clarksdale Carnegie Library','Clarksdale','ms','civic','standing',34.20377,-90.57488,'exact','open','walk','','1913–1914',
  "Lillian M. Waddell of the women's club wrote to Carnegie's secretary in 1909, and the library by Charles O. Pfeil of Memphis, in a Jacobethan and Gothic Revival style, opened on April 14, 1914. The county joined the city in paying for it in 1917, and the county's first bookmobile ran from here from 1923. It has been extended, and it is still the Carnegie Public Library of Clarksdale and Coahoma County, with a genealogy room and a display of Native American pottery.",
  'Jacobethan and Gothic Revival.',
  '','114 Delta Avenue, at 1st Street. A working public library.',
  [['Carnegie Public Library of Clarksdale: about','https://www.cplclarksdale.lib.ms.us/about/'],['HMdb: Carnegie Public Library','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=89927'],LM],brick='?',hours='Mon–Thu 9–5:30, Fri 9–5, Sat 9–1'),
N('greenwood-carnegie-library-ms','Greenwood Carnegie Library','Greenwood','ms','civic','standing',33.51905,-90.18483,'area','view','walk','','1913–1914',
  "A mildly Jacobean Revival library of red brick in Flemish bond with stone window frames and belt courses, built in 1913 and 1914, perhaps to designs by Frank R. McGeoy; its first 800 books came from the collection of J. Z. George. Murals were painted inside in 1935, and a wing of about 1955 tripled its size. The library moved across the street in the 1970s. What the building is used for now was not found. A Mississippi Landmark.",
  'Red brick in Flemish bond with stone trim.',
  '','408 West Washington Street, at Henderson Street. Look from the street.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Greenwood library','https://archipediavm.upress.virginia.edu/buildings/MS-02-DR49'],['KSL: Greenwood library celebrating 100 years','https://www.ksl.com/article/32039732/greenwood-library-celebrating-100-years'],LM],brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('gulfport-carnegie-library-ms','Gulfport Carnegie Library (Gulfport Arts Center)','Gulfport','ms','civic','standing',30.36717,-89.09123,'exact','open','walk','','1916–1917',
  "The only Carnegie library on the Mississippi coast, by Nolan and Torre, opened in April 1917: buff brick with big round-arched windows, Corinthian pilasters and a balustrade in the parapet. The library left in 1966. It came through Katrina, and since 2015 it has been the Gulfport Arts Center, run by a non-profit, renovated with state and city money in 2024.",
  'Buff brick with round-arched windows and Corinthian pilasters.',
  '','1300 24th Avenue. The arts center; signs on the downstairs doors say to call for help getting in.',
  [['Gulfport Arts Center','https://www.gulfportartscenter.com/'],['SAH Archipedia: Gulfport Carnegie Library','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/MS-02-GC16'],['Harrison County Library System: history','https://www.harrison.lib.ms.us/?p=2014'],LM],
  brick='no',bcol='buff',hours='Wed–Sun 11–5'),
N('houston-carnegie-library-ms','Houston Carnegie Library','Houston','ms','civic','standing',33.89643,-89.00103,'exact','open','walk','','1908–1909',
  "Mississippi's first Carnegie library, built in 1909 after the schools superintendent L. B. Reid wrote to Carnegie, having seen a Carnegie library near Tyler, Texas. It is a one-story brick building in the shape of a cross under a metal hip roof, mixing Prairie and Stick styles, with a 16-pane skylight over the centre hall and a bracketed canopy over the door. It is still the library. On the National Register since 1978.",
  'A cross-shaped plan under a metal roof, with a bracketed canopy over the door.',
  '','105 West Madison Street, at Huddleston Street. A working public library.',
  [W('Houston Carnegie Library'),['City of Houston: library','https://houston.ms.gov/library'],LM],brick='?',hours='Tue–Fri 9–5, Sat 9–12'),
N('meridian-carnegie-library-ms','Meridian Carnegie Library (Meridian Museum of Art)','Meridian','ms','civic','standing',32.36396,-88.70325,'exact','open','walk','','1913',
  "Library commission chairman Israel Marks won $38,000 from Carnegie, $30,000 for this library and $8,000 for a branch for Black readers, also on this map. Wikipedia says this building was the First Presbyterian Church, rebuilt in brick after an 1883 fire and bought by the city in 1911; the state survey dates it 1912 to 1913 and says nothing of a church. It opened as the library in 1913. Its brick is painted white. The library left in 1967, and the Meridian Museum of Art opened here in 1970. On the National Register since 1979.",
  'Brick painted white.',
  '','628 25th Avenue, at 7th Street. The museum\'s hours were not found on a page of its own.',
  [W('Meridian Museum of Art'),['Mississippi Department of Archives and History: survey form','https://www.apps.mdah.ms.gov/nom/prop/16646.pdf'],LM],brick='no',bcol='painted',hours='?'),
N('meridian-carnegie-branch-library-site-ms','Site of the Meridian Carnegie Branch Library (13th Street Colored Branch)','Meridian','ms','civic','gone',32.36973,-88.70651,'area','view','walk','','1912–2008',
  "Mississippi's first library for African Americans, a one-story red brick building on a raised basement, opened in March 1913 with $8,000 from Carnegie on land given by Black Methodists. Dr. J. Beverly Shaw chaired its advisory board. It closed in 1974 as no longer fit for public use, served later as a Head Start office, and was torn down on May 28, 2008, after a plan to make it an arts centre failed. Only part of its front walk remains. A state marker of 2018 stands at 13th Street and 28th Avenue.",
  'Part of the front walk, and a state marker.',
  '','2721 13th Street. Nothing of the building stands.',
  [W('Carnegie Branch Library (Meridian, Mississippi)'),['BlackPast: 13th Street Colored Branch Library','https://blackpast.org/african-american-history/13th-street-colored-branch-library-meridian-mississippi-1913-1974'],['HMdb: Carnegie Branch Library','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=140955'],LM],brick='?'),
N('mound-bayou-carnegie-library-site-ms','Site of the Mound Bayou Carnegie Library','Mound Bayou','ms','civic','gone',33.87846,-90.72678,'area','view','walk','','1910–1935',
  "BlackPast calls it the only Carnegie public library grant to a Black town. Charles Banks asked Carnegie in 1909 and $4,000 was offered three weeks later. William Sidney Pittman, Booker T. Washington's son-in-law, designed a one-story red brick building, opened in December 1910 with newspapers and magazines; it was used for meetings, farmers' conferences and classes, and Wikipedia's list says it was never used as a library. It burned in the 1935 fire that took much of the business district.",
  '',
  '','The southwest corner of Green and Fisher streets, opposite the old Normal and Industrial Institute. Nothing of it stands.',
  [['BlackPast: Mound Bayou Carnegie Library','https://new.blackpast.org/?p=66990'],LM],brick='?'),
N('okolona-carnegie-library-ms','Okolona Carnegie Library','Okolona','ms','civic','standing',34.00438,-88.75094,'exact','open','walk','','1914–1915',
  "Attorney A. T. Stovall asked for the grant in February 1914, and the library by John Gaisford of Memphis was finished in May 1915: one large room over a full basement. An addition of 1986 tripled the shelf space. A story labyrinth stands in front. It is still the library, and a Mississippi Landmark.",
  'A story labyrinth in front.',
  '','321 West Main Street. A working public library.',
  [W('Okolona Carnegie Library'),['City of Okolona: library','https://cityofokolona.com/library'],LM],brick='?',hours='Mon, Tue, Thu, Fri 9:30–4:30; Sat 9–12'),
N('vicksburg-carnegie-library-ms','Vicksburg Carnegie Library','Vicksburg','ms','civic','standing',32.34816,-90.88031,'exact','view','walk','','1914–1916',
  "A two-story Mission Revival library by Edward L. Tilton of New York, finished in February 1916: stucco walls on a brick foundation, a red tile roof, curving parapets, a Doric doorway and round-arched fanlights. The Vicksburg Post calls it the town's only Mediterranean-style commercial building. The library left in 1979 and city offices use it now. A Mississippi Landmark.",
  'Stucco, curving parapets and a red tile roof.',
  '','819 South Street, at Monroe Street. City offices: look from the street.',
  [['National Register nomination: Old Vicksburg Public Library','https://www.apps.mdah.ms.gov/nom/prop/27134.pdf'],['Vicksburg Post','https://www.vicksburgpost.com/?p=614656'],LM],brick='no'),
N('west-point-carnegie-library-ms','West Point Carnegie Library','West Point','ms','civic','standing',33.60474,-88.64613,'area','view','walk','','1913–',
  "West Point's $10,000 grant was made in January 1913. Archipedia says the Chamber of Commerce uses the building; Wikipedia's list says a community development group. When it was built and by whom were not found.",
  '',
  '','510 East Broad Street. Look from the street.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: West Point Carnegie Library','https://archipediavm.upress.virginia.edu/buildings/MS-02-PR12'],LM],brick='?'),
